## Reviewer Report

### 🔴 Critical (must fix before merge)
None found.

### 🟠 Major (strongly recommended)
- `hooks/tests/verify-red-green.test.sh:158` — `test_relative_verify_cmd()` invokes the script with `--range "$base..$head"`, but the script's own usage synopsis (unchanged context lines in the diff, `scripts/verify-red-green.sh:10-11` / `platforms/codex/plugin/scripts/verify-red-green.sh:10-11`) documents only `--base <ref> --head <ref> --verify-cmd <script-path> [--test-glob <glob>]... [--repo <dir>]` — no `--range` flag appears anywhere in the diff's usage text. **UNVERIFIED** (live-system fact — I could not execute the script or view the un-diffed argument-parsing loop, which is outside the provided hunks and outside tool access in this review session): if `--range` is not a recognized flag, `err_usage` fires and the script exits 2 with no JSON, which would fail the first assertion (`assert_eq "$ec" "0" ...`) outright. Note the second assertion in the same test (`nonexistent relative verify-cmd exits 2`) is **not** discriminating either way — an unsupported `--range` flag *also* exits 2, so that assertion would pass "for the wrong reason" even if the intended fix path is never exercised. Consequence: either (a) this regression test fails in CI as written, or (b) `--range` is a real undocumented alias and the usage docstring is stale — both need to be resolved before merge. Fix direction: run `bash hooks/tests/run.sh` (or directly `scripts/verify-red-green.sh --range "a..b" --verify-cmd foo --repo bar`) to confirm; if unsupported, change the test to use `--base "$base" --head "$head"` to match the documented interface; if supported, add `--range` to the usage() docstring for consistency.

### 🟡 Minor (recommended)
- Review-process disclosure, not a code defect: step 7 of the reviewer workflow requires running `bash hooks/tests/run.sh` as a pre-merge gate; **tool execution was unavailable in this review session**, so the test suite was not run and the Major finding above could not be confirmed by execution. This bound must be closed by actually running the suite before merge (see `references/blind-dispatch.md` "No silent caps").

### 🔵 Suggestion (consider)
- `scripts/verify-red-green.sh:52-75` / `platforms/codex/plugin/scripts/verify-red-green.sh:52-75` — `json_escape()`'s per-character bash loop (`for ((i=0; i<${#s}; i++))`) is O(n) with a high per-character constant (case match + `printf -v` per char). Fine for the expected input domain (branch refs, short paths, short reason strings) but would be slow if ever fed a large string. Consider documenting the assumed input-size bound, or note it's intentionally traded off against the "no new dependency" constraint stated in the comment.
- `scripts/verify-red-green.sh:51-52` / same in the codex copy — `usage()` relies on a hardcoded `sed -n '2,25p'` line range tied exactly to the header comment's line count. This is fragile: a future edit that grows/shrinks the header comment without updating both numeric bounds (in both platform copies) will silently truncate or overflow the printed usage text with no test catching it. Consider a sentinel marker (e.g., `# --- END USAGE ---`) and `sed -n '/^#/{...}'`-style extraction instead of magic line numbers.
- `scripts/verify-red-green.sh` and `platforms/codex/plugin/scripts/verify-red-green.sh` are maintained as two manually-synced files (confirmed identical blob hashes `44f03ee`→`7d07b20` pre- and post-diff on both). There is no visible CI check enforcing the two stay byte-identical; a future PR touching only one copy would silently drift. Consider a checksum/diff CI check, or replacing one with a symlink to the other.
- `hooks/tests/verify-red-green.test.sh:180-182` — `test_json_escape_control_chars` unit-tests `json_escape` by `sed`-extracting the function body and `eval`-ing it directly into the test shell, while the sibling `test_relative_verify_cmd` (and presumably the rest of the suite) black-box tests via full script invocation + `json_field`. This mixes unit and integration testing styles in the same file. Not a defect, but for consistency consider exercising `json_escape` indirectly through a full script run with a crafted `reason`/path string containing control characters, matching the rest of the suite's black-box convention.

### ✅ Verified Clean
- Reviewed `json_escape()` rewrite (`scripts/verify-red-green.sh:56-77`, identical in the codex copy) character-by-character against `hooks/tests/verify-red-green.test.sh:180-183`'s input `a\nb\tc\rd\be\ff"g\\h\x01i` — traced every case branch (backslash, quote, `\n \t \r \b \f`, and the generic `ord<32` → `\u00XX` fallback) and confirmed it produces the exact expected `a\nb\tc\rd\be\ff\"g\\h\u0001i`. No bug found in the escaping logic itself; multi-byte/locale behavior does not affect correctness because bytes ≥0x80 always fail the `ord<32` test regardless of whether `${s:i:1}` splits on full codepoints or raw bytes.
- Reviewed relative `--verify-cmd` canonicalization (`scripts/verify-red-green.sh:183-192`, identical in the codex copy): absolute paths (`/*`) pass through unchanged (backward compatible with existing CI callers); relative paths are resolved via `cd "$(dirname ...)" && pwd` + `basename` *before* the `-x` executable check and before any later `cd` into a worktree; a nonexistent relative path produces a clear `err_usage` (exit 2) rather than a silent `NOT_GREEN_ON_HEAD` misclassification, which matches the stated regression this fix addresses.
- Confirmed both platform copies (`scripts/verify-red-green.sh`, `platforms/codex/plugin/scripts/verify-red-green.sh`) received byte-identical diff hunks — both files' pre-image and post-image git blob hashes (`44f03ee`→`7d07b20`) match, so the two duplicated scripts remain in sync after this change.
- Reviewed `usage()` sed-range change (`2,21p`→`2,25p`) against the +4-line header comment insertion — the range delta matches the added line count exactly.
- No hardcoded secrets, no SQL/XSS/CSRF surface (pure bash CLI script), no command-injection introduced by the canonicalization (uses quoted `[[ ]]`, `dirname`/`basename` as literal-argument coreutils calls, no `eval`/`sh -c` on user input in the changed hunks).
- LLM/agent-dispatch threat checklist (model-output-as-data, prompt injection, excessive agency, unbounded consumption) — not applicable; this diff is a git-verification bash script, not an agent-dispatch path.
- Reviewed full diff for scope creep — every changed line traces to the task (fix relative `--verify-cmd` resolution + fix incomplete `json_escape` control-character coverage, applied identically to both platform copies, plus the accompanying doc-comment and regression tests). No unrelated renames, reformatting, or refactors found.
